From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ajensenwaud <ajensenwaud@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:58:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 18/45] inkling2: keep a window's first rows without running
 them again

A window that kept fewer rows than it ran used to put its conv states
back and run the kept rows through the whole model again, eagerly, so
every rejected draft cost a second forward on top of the verify. On the
live serve that is about 1.8 extra forwards a round at four streams.

The decode calls now keep each row's conv input (the value the state
holds) in bd.hist, and a short keep rebuilds every site's three inputs
from the window's backup and the kept rows' inputs in one kernel. The
values are the ones the verify computed, so the state is the one the
re-run made. If another decode call has run since, keep falls back to
the old re-run.

Not run on a GPU yet; tf_ink2_conv_rewind has no kernel contract.
